## AI Engineering Platform — Cursor Rules

You are an expert TypeScript/Python/React engineer working on the AI Engineering Platform (AIEP), a production system for orchestrating LLM workflows and AI observability.

---

## Context Loading

Before acting on any task, load these files:
- `.ai/instructions/instruction-hierarchy.md` (governance)
- `.ai/instructions/global-rules.md` (rules)
- `.ai/memory/current-architecture.md` (system state)
- Task-relevant skill from `.ai/skills/`

---

## Technology Stack

- TypeScript + Node.js 20 + Fastify (backend services)
- Python 3.12 + FastAPI (AI/ML services)
- React 18 + TypeScript + Vite (frontend)
- PostgreSQL + Redis + Weaviate + Kafka
- Kubernetes + Terraform + GitHub Actions

---

## Code Rules

### TypeScript
- Strict mode always on — no `any` without justified comment
- Result<T, E> pattern for fallible business logic
- Zod validation at all external boundaries
- Named exports over default exports
- Dependency injection via constructors

### Python
- Type hints required on all public functions
- Pydantic v2 for data models
- ruff + black formatting
- No bare `except:` clauses

### React
- Functional components only
- Custom hooks for reusable logic (`use` prefix)
- CSS modules or Tailwind — no inline styles
- All route-level components have error boundaries

---

## Architecture Constraints

- Services never directly access another service's database
- Auth handled exclusively by auth-service
- Secrets from environment variables only
- New API endpoints follow `docs/API_CONVENTIONS.md`

---

## Testing Requirements

- New code requires tests
- Test files co-located: `foo.ts` → `foo.test.ts`
- Unit tests mock all I/O
- Test names: `should [verb] [outcome] when [condition]`

---

## Security (Non-Negotiable)

- No hardcoded secrets or credentials
- All SQL uses parameterized queries
- User input never passed to eval/exec/shell
- Auth middleware applied before data access
- Error responses never expose stack traces

---

## What NOT to Generate

- Code bypassing auth or authorization
- Logging that includes passwords, tokens, or PII
- Hardcoded environment-specific values
- SQL with string interpolation
- `@ts-ignore` without explanatory comment
- Empty catch blocks

---

